sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa02-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The thicker and hotter an animal's blood, the more it conduces to strength; the thinner and colder, the better suited it is to sensation and intelligence; those with hot, thin and clear blood are best for both courage and intelligence.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Thick hot blood gives strength; thin cool blood gives sensation and intelligence";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book II, Part 2; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: animals with blood, and by analogy bloodless animals"
}
